@description REAPER MCP — Model Context Protocol interface (immersive/spatial audio)
@version 1.16.0
@author James Livingston
@link https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet
@provides
  [darwin] reaper_mcp.dylib https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet/releases/download/v$version/reaper_mcp.dylib
@changelog
  The meter was measuring at your sound card's rate, not your project's -- and the ADM export was shipping files labelled with it.

  REAPER's render engine follows the audio device. Unless you have asked REAPER to request a
  specific rate, the device runs at whatever rate it likes, and every render follows it -- including
  the temporary renders this extension uses to measure loudness and to author deliverables. If your
  project says 48 kHz and your interface is running at 44.1, the numbers you were reading, and the
  files you were writing, were 44.1.

  Nothing was lying. `analysis.meter` reported its figures honestly, `spatial.export_adm` wrote the
  rate it actually rendered at into both the WAV header and the ADM metadata, and the project setting
  reported the project setting. There was simply nothing anywhere that compared the two numbers.

  WHAT CHANGES. The analysis and deliverable renders are now REQUESTED at the project's sample rate
  instead of following the engine. On a correctly configured machine nothing changes at all. On a
  machine where the device is running at another rate:

    * `analysis.meter` now measures what your project actually is. True peak is the figure that
      moves -- it is oversampled from the rendered samples, so the rate it was rendered at matters.
    * `spatial.export_adm` with `profile: "dolby-atmos"` stops refusing a project that is already at
      48 kHz. It used to refuse with "set the project sample rate to 48000", which was no help if you
      had already done exactly that.
    * `spatial.export_adm` on its default settings stops silently writing a master at the wrong rate.
      It now warns when the rendered rate is not the project's, and every rate refusal in the
      extension names the real cause -- including your audio device's rate when it can read it.

  `analysis.meter` and `analysis.revive_and_meter` also report `rate.requested` (what the render was
  asked for) and `rate.device` (your audio interface's own rate, when REAPER's device is open), so
  you can see where a disagreement comes from rather than guessing.

  IF YOU WANT THE OLD BEHAVIOUR, ASK FOR IT BY NAME. Both tools take `renderRate`: `"project"` is the
  new default, `"follow"` is the previous behaviour, and a number forces a specific rate. Nothing is
  taken away.

  ONE THING THIS DOES NOT FIX. Forcing the render rate means REAPER resamples. That is the right
  trade for a measurement that has to describe your deliverable, but it is a resample, and the
  extension says so in a warning rather than leaving you to work it out.

  No tools were added or removed.
